idle: don't reset the idle clock when setInhibit changes nothing

recheckIdleInhibitorStatus() runs on every window map (Window.cpp:1494),
unmap (:1687), focus change (FocusState.cpp:151, :220) and fullscreen toggle
(FullscreenController.cpp:501). With no idle inhibitors and no `idleinhibit`
window rules it always falls through to setInhibit(false) — which then calls
update() on every ext-idle-notification regardless of whether the inhibit
state actually changed. update(0) calls reset() (sending `resumed`) and rearms
the full timeout, so a false → false recheck silently rewinds the idle clock.

Consequence: any background window that opens, closes or takes focus resets
the compositor's idle timer. A Steam notification toast (an unmanaged X11
window, class steam, title notificationtoasts_NN_desktop) is enough — hypridle
receives `resumed` and runs its on-resume, so a locked, DPMS-off session lights
its panels back up hours later with the lock screen on them. The other half is
worse and invisible: while such popups arrive faster than the lock timeout, the
idle lock can never fire at all.

Measured on this machine: 81 Steam popups over two days; 11 of 11 spontaneous
DPMS-on events each land 220-880ms after a PopupHTMLWindow, while every wake
caused by real input has no popup near it.

A genuine true → false transition still rearms the timers, so releasing a real
inhibitor is unaffected.

Applies to Hyprland 52b368f1d7fdee1b0e96ef3dc655b5a577df4fdb (v0.56.0).

--- a/src/protocols/IdleNotify.cpp
+++ b/src/protocols/IdleNotify.cpp
@@ -116,6 +116,12 @@
 }
 
